[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Hoisting in JavaScript"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Hoisting is a JavaScript mechanism where variable and function declarations are moved to the top of their scope before code execution. It's important to understand that only the declarations are hoisted, not the initializations."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Variable Hoisting"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"When using 'var', variables are hoisted to the top of their scope and initialized with 'undefined'. This means you can use a variable before it's declared in your code, but its value will be 'undefined'."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"console.log(myVar); // Output: undefined
var myVar = 10;
console.log(myVar); // Output: 10"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"With 'let' and 'const', variables are also hoisted, but they are not initialized. They are in a "temporal dead zone" (TDZ) until their declaration is evaluated. Accessing them before the declaration results in a 'ReferenceError'."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"console.log(myLet); // Output: ReferenceError: Cannot access 'myLet' before initialization
let myLet = 20;
console.log(myLet); // Output: 20"},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"console.log(myConst); // Output: ReferenceError: Cannot access 'myConst' before initialization
const myConst = 30;
console.log(myConst); // Output: 30"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Function Hoisting"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Function declarations are hoisted completely, meaning both the declaration and the definition are moved to the top of the scope. This allows you to call a function before it's defined in your code."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"myFunction(); // Output: Hello from myFunction

function myFunction() {
  console.log('Hello from myFunction');
}"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Function expressions, however, are treated like variable declarations. If you use 'var' to declare a function expression, the variable will be hoisted but the function definition will not. If you use 'let' or 'const', the function expression will not be accessible before its declaration."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"myFunctionExpression(); // Output: TypeError: myFunctionExpression is not a function

var myFunctionExpression = function() {
  console.log('Hello from myFunctionExpression');
};"},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"myLetFunctionExpression(); // Output: ReferenceError: Cannot access 'myLetFunctionExpression' before initialization

let myLetFunctionExpression = function() {
  console.log('Hello from myLetFunctionExpression');
};"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Practical Implications and Best Practices"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"While hoisting can be useful, it can also lead to unexpected behavior and bugs if not understood properly."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"To avoid confusion, it's generally recommended to declare variables and functions at the top of their scope."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Using 'let' and 'const' instead of 'var' can help prevent issues related to variable hoisting, as they provide clearer error messages when accessed before declaration."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Be mindful of the differences between function declarations and function expressions when it comes to hoisting."},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"By understanding hoisting, you can write more predictable and maintainable JavaScript code."}]
